    {
      "kind": "topic",
      "text": "### The \"Why\" of Deltas",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000044"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source",
      "chunkId": "c0001-flat-03",
      "annotations": [
        "repair_source_fallback"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "source",
      "text": "Deltas are not just numbers; they are the cost of crossing a scope. When a variable moves out of one lambda and into another, it gains depth. Deltas ensure that the interaction net \"knows\" where its variables live without ever scanning the code.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000045"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source",
      "chunkId": "c0001-flat-03",
      "annotations": [
        "repair_source_fallback"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "topic",
      "text": "### How connections resolve",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000046"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source",
      "chunkId": "c0001-flat-03",
      "annotations": [
        "repair_source_fallback"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "source",
      "text": "When an application node (the @) meets a λ-abstraction, they interact. The λ-node's binding port determines if it needs to replicate the variable (using a Replicator node) or erase it (if it doesn't appear in the body).",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000047"
      ],
