      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "source",
      "text": "A reduction order says which redex to simplify next. For erasure, normal order—outermost application before its argument—can avoid reducing something a function is about to throw away. Sharing is harder. The paper follows Lévy: some shared terms have no ordinary sequential order that avoids every duplicated reduction.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000075"
